# ADR: Workaround ADRs need an enforced re-check trigger, not just a stated one

**Status:** Accepted
**Date:** 2026-09-08
**Renumber to fit the existing `/docs/adr-NNN-*.md` sequence before committing.**

## Context

adr-004 (2026-09-03) accepted a workaround and explicitly recommended
root-causing "before a second consumer of transaction_time is built." HAHA
Sales Ingestion became that second consumer on 2026-09-07. Nobody reopened
adr-004 when it happened. It took a production incident four days later,
and an explicit instruction in this session's kickoff brief to check, for
the condition to actually get evaluated.

The stated condition was correct. It just had no mechanism forcing anyone
to check it at the moment it became true.

This is the same shape of failure as adr-001 (a transplanted pattern's
precondition not re-derived at the destination) and adr-003 (a resilience
fix in one flow not checked against its siblings), just at the level of
ADR governance instead of workflow wiring. A written condition that
depends on someone remembering to check it is not different in kind from
a named node reference that depends on someone remembering it might not
have executed.

## Decision

Any ADR accepted with Status "Accepted (workaround)" — a decision that
names a condition under which it should be reopened — gets that condition
logged as a standing item in the DoD sweep, not left as prose inside the
ADR alone. When a build session's DoD sweep touches a system the condition
references, checking whether the condition has fired is part of the sweep,
not optional.

This does not require tooling. It requires the condition to be checked in
the same place the rest of the DoD sweep already gets checked, alongside
locked business rules and cross-flow propagation.

## Consequences

**Positive:**
- Converts "someone should remember to reopen this" into a routine check,
  the same fix already applied to fix propagation in adr-003.
- Costs nothing to add — the DoD sweep already exists and already reads
  ADRs for locked rules.

**Negative:**
- Only as good as the DoD sweep actually running. If the sweep gets
  skipped, so does this — same limitation adr-003 already accepted for
  its own check.
- Adds one more thing to hold in mind during every sweep, on a list that
  already has several.

## Related

- Prompted directly by adr-006 (root-cause transaction_time), whose
  triggering condition sat unchecked for five days.
- Same class of failure as adr-001 (unre-derived precondition) and
  adr-003 (unpropagated fix), generalized to ADR governance itself.
